      "Gati Shakti Multi-Modal Cargo Terminals (GCTs) are railway cargo terminals developed under the Ministry of Railways' GCT Policy 2021 to accelerate modern terminal development and encourage private participation, refreshed by a Master Circular in 2026 ([Indian Railways GCT Master Circular 2026](https://indianrailways.gov.in/railwayboard/uploads/directorate/traffic_comm/Master_Circulars/2026/GCT%20Master%20Circular%202026%20(1).pdf)). Indian Railways reported in January 2026 that 306 GCTs had been approved with a combined capacity of about 192 million tonnes per annum, of which 118 were already commissioned ([PIB, 13 January 2026](https://pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2214076&reg=3&lang=1)). For a shipper, the practical question is narrow: is there a GCT that can accept my commodity, within a workable road distance of my plant, with a rake plan that fits my volumes?",
      "## What a Gati Shakti Cargo Terminal is",
      "A GCT is a terminal with direct rail connectivity where freight can be aggregated, loaded into rakes and dispatched, or received and distributed onward by road. The policy framework also covers Cargo Related Facilities (CRFs), which support terminal operations at a smaller scale. GCTs sit under the PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan and are part of the same multimodal logic that includes Dedicated Freight Corridors, Multi-Modal Logistics Parks and the National Logistics Policy ([IBEF, September 2026](https://www.ibef.org/blogs/green-logistics-in-india-developments-and-opportunities)).",
      "## Why a shipper should care",
